# =============================================================================
# Dang, It's Hot: every setting the web app reads (apps/web)
# =============================================================================
#
# WHERE THEY GO
#   Live site: vercel.com → your project → Settings → Environment Variables.
#   Add each one with "Production" ticked, then Deployments → the top one →
#   ⋯ → Redeploy. A change only takes effect after a redeploy.
#   Your own computer: copy this file to apps/web/.env.local.
#
# Every value here is deliberately EMPTY. Fill in your own; never paste this
# file in as it is. Old template values ("generate-…", "your-…", "re_xxx",
# "sk_test_xxx") count as not set, and the deploy log and /ops/launch name
# any that are left.
#
# CHECKING: docs/going-live.md is the step-by-step. Once you can sign in,
# /ops/launch checks every one of these on the live site, red/amber/green,
# with the fix for each.
#
# Three groups:
#   1. REQUIRED FOR GO-LIVE: all eight, before the first real customer.
#   2. OPTIONAL: card payments, texts, address lookup, and so on.
#   3. NEVER ON THE LIVE SITE: demo and developer switches. Leave them out of
#      Vercel completely.


# =============================================================================
# 1. REQUIRED FOR GO-LIVE
# =============================================================================

# --- The database (Supabase). Without both, the site runs in demo mode: the
# quote form works but NOTHING is saved. Then bring the database up to date
# once: paste supabase/pilot-upgrade.sql into Supabase → SQL Editor → Run
# (docs/going-live.md step 2).
# Supabase → Project Settings → API → Project URL (https://….supabase.co)
SUPABASE_URL=
# Supabase → Project Settings → API → the service_role (secret) key. It opens
# the whole database: only ever in Vercel, never in an email or a chat.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Sign-in security. Signs everyone's sign-in (and the upload links).
# Missing, shorter than 32 characters, or a template value: production signs
# NOBODY in. Make one: on a Mac open Terminal, type
#     openssl rand -hex 32
# press Enter, and copy the 64 letters and numbers it prints.
# Changing it later signs everyone out (they just sign in again).
SESSION_SECRET=

# --- The first admin. Your own email address(es), comma separated. The first
# sign-in from one of these creates its admin account; it's the only way to
# get the first admin. Add the rest of the team on /ops/staff.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# --- Email (Resend). Carries the sign-in links, the proposal email (the
# pre-contract information and the cancellation form, which the law says
# must reach the customer), the contract copy and every customer update.
# Without it the live site refuses to issue proposals.
# resend.com → API Keys → Create API key.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Who emails come from, on a domain you've verified in resend.com → Domains,
# e.g.  Dang It's Hot <hello@yourdomain.co.uk>
# (Replies go to the company email in /ops/settings, so make that a real,
# watched inbox: customers asking for an early start reply to it.)
EMAIL_FROM=

# --- The live web address, with https:// and no slash at the end, e.g.
# https://dang.ac. Every link in every email points here. Redeploy after
# changing it (it's built into the pages).
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=

# --- The daily jobs in apps/web/vercel.json: survey and day-before
# reminders, proposal expiry, message retries and aftercare (16:00 UTC), the
# morning digest (06:00) and the abandoned-quote nudge (10:00). Vercel sends
# it to them by itself. Without it they all refuse to run.
# Make one the same way:  openssl rand -hex 32
CRON_SECRET=


# =============================================================================
# 2. OPTIONAL
# =============================================================================

# --- Break-glass admin sign-in, for when email is down. With it, /login also
# accepts an ADMIN_EMAILS address plus this password (a 12-hour session,
# logged and rate limited). At least 20 characters or it stays off. Make one:
#     openssl rand -base64 24
# Leave it out for email links only.
OPS_PASSWORD=

# --- Where new-lead and lost-lead alerts go. Empty = the EMAIL_FROM address.
LEADS_NOTIFY_EMAIL=

# --- Card payments (Stripe Checkout). Empty = bank transfer only, recorded
# by hand on the project's money page. sk_test_… first (no real money, the
# site shows TEST), then sk_live_…. stripe.com → Developers → API keys.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Money is only ever recorded from Stripe's signed webhook. stripe.com →
# Developers → Webhooks → Add endpoint:
#     https://<your-domain>/api/stripe/webhook
# with these events: checkout.session.completed,
# checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded,
# checkout.session.async_payment_failed, charge.refunded,
# charge.refund.updated, charge.dispute.created, charge.dispute.closed.
# Then copy its "Signing secret" (whsec_…) here.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- Texts (Twilio): survey and day-before reminders, "on my way". Empty =
# every text is written and kept in /ops/messages as "demo, not sent";
# customers still get every email. twilio.com → Console → Account info.
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# The sender: DangItsHot (letters only, up to 11; customers can't reply to
# it, so every text includes our phone number) or a Twilio number like
# +447700900000.
TWILIO_FROM=

# --- Address lookup on the quote form. Known addresses come from the
# property database first; getAddress.io fills the gaps. Empty = customers
# type their address.
GETADDRESS_API_KEY=

# --- Video walkthroughs (the phone capture app; not part of the pilot).
# Their public endpoints are off on the live site unless this is 1.
VIDEO_SURVEYS_ENABLED=
# Only matter with VIDEO_SURVEYS_ENABLED=1. Without them videos still upload
# and wait for a person to look at them.
# OpenAI: transcribes the narration.
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# Anthropic: turns the transcript into a floor plan.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=


# =============================================================================
# 3. NEVER ON THE LIVE SITE
# =============================================================================
# Demo and developer switches, commented out on purpose. Leave them out of
# Vercel's Production settings completely. /ops/launch and the deploy log
# both flag a demo switch that's on.

# "Try as…" demo accounts on /login (one per role) and the "Load demo pilots"
# button. A demo admin can see and change everything, so: on in development;
# on a live deployment only with ALLOW_DEMO_LOGIN=1; with Supabase connected
# also only with DEMO_DEPLOYMENT=1; and never, whatever these say, on a
# database that holds a real quote, customer or team member.
# ALLOW_DEMO_LOGIN=
# DEMO_DEPLOYMENT=

# Keep records in files or memory instead of Supabase: a developer's own
# computer only. On Vercel every server has its own copy, so records vanish.
# "file" (the default on your own computer) keeps JSON per table and uploads
# under LOCAL_DATA_DIR (default apps/web/.data, never committed); "memory"
# forgets everything on restart; "none" forces demo mode.
# DATA_BACKEND=
# LOCAL_DATA_DIR=
# Rows per read on the local backend (default 1000, as Supabase), for tests.
# LOCAL_MAX_ROWS=


# =============================================================================
# SCRIPTS AND TESTS ONLY (your computer or GitHub; never Vercel)
# =============================================================================

# EPC Open Data API, for scripts/fetch-epc.mjs and the "Load property data"
# GitHub workflow (add them there as repository secrets). Free sign-up at
# https://epc.opendatacommunities.org/. EPC_API_TOKEN is the alternative:
# the base64 of "email:key", ready made.
# EPC_API_EMAIL=
# EPC_API_KEY=
# EPC_API_TOKEN=

# The end-to-end browser walks (pnpm --filter @aircon/web test:e2e and
# test:e2e:pilot): the site to test, the Chromium to drive, where to save
# screenshots, and (pilot walk) the section to stop after.
# BASE_URL=
# CHROMIUM_PATH=
# PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=
# SHOTS_DIR=
# E2E_UNTIL=

# Set by Next.js and Vercel themselves; never set these: NODE_ENV, NEXT_RUNTIME.
